SAT_CHUNK("rw/cross-text/3/1",[
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.b3a6c5cba1","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-21","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some researchers argue that allowing students to choose which books they read in school increases how much they read. When a group of middle schools replaced assigned novels with a large classroom library, students could pick any book as long as they wrote a short reflection afterward.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A two-year study of middle schools found that students given free choice of books did read more pages overall. However, the same students were less likely to attempt books above their current reading level, and their scores on tests of challenging vocabulary grew more slowly than those of students who also read some assigned texts.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that reflections are more important than the books students read","By claiming that students given choice actually read less than students given assigned books","By suggesting that assigned novels should be removed from all middle schools","By agreeing that choice may increase reading but cautioning that it can come with drawbacks for building advanced skills"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Text 2 confirms students read more pages but notes they avoided harder books and gained challenging vocabulary more slowly. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.49c94bf396","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-21","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some researchers argue that allowing students to choose which books they read in school increases how much they read. When a group of middle schools replaced assigned novels with a large classroom library, students could pick any book as long as they wrote a short reflection afterward.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A two-year study of middle schools found that students given free choice of books did read more pages overall. However, the same students were less likely to attempt books above their current reading level, and their scores on tests of challenging vocabulary grew more slowly than those of students who also read some assigned texts.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes a difference in how the authors of Text 1 and Text 2 view student choice in reading?","choices":["Text 1 presents it as a benefit, while Text 2 suggests that its benefits may be accompanied by costs.","Text 1 focuses on high school students, while Text 2 focuses on college students.","Text 1 relies on test scores, while Text 2 relies on students' reflections.","Text 1 argues that it lowers reading volume, while Text 2 argues that it raises reading volume."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 1 emphasizes increased reading; Text 2 adds evidence of drawbacks. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.737b261c90","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-22","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many conservationists favor protecting large, continuous areas of habitat, arguing that big reserves support more species and allow animals to roam, find mates, and adapt to changing conditions.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Large reserves have clear advantages, but a network of many smaller protected areas can sometimes preserve more total species. Because small reserves can be spread across different habitats, such as wetlands, grasslands, and forests, they may capture a wider range of plants and animals than a single large reserve located in one type of landscape.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that small reserves are always better than large reserves","By rejecting the idea that animals need to roam or find mates","By acknowledging the advantages of large reserves while arguing that several smaller reserves may sometimes protect more species","By arguing that reserves should be located only in forests"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 2 grants \"clear advantages\" but proposes that networks of small reserves can capture more habitats and species. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4755849407","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-24","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers claim that eating late at night inevitably leads to weight gain, because the body supposedly burns fewer calories while sleeping.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Controlled studies that give participants the same total calories at different times of day have generally found little difference in weight change. Late-night eating may be linked to weight gain in surveys, but that link often reflects what people eat at night, such as high-calorie snacks, and how much they eat overall, not the timing itself.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By suggesting that surveys about eating habits are always accurate","By agreeing that the body burns no calories during sleep","By arguing that the total amount and type of food people eat matter more than the time at which they eat it","By claiming that eating at night causes weight loss"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 2 cites studies showing timing has little effect when calories are equal and points to what and how much people eat. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6defd891d4","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-24","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers claim that eating late at night inevitably leads to weight gain, because the body supposedly burns fewer calories while sleeping.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Controlled studies that give participants the same total calories at different times of day have generally found little difference in weight change. Late-night eating may be linked to weight gain in surveys, but that link often reflects what people eat at night, such as high-calorie snacks, and how much they eat overall, not the timing itself.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the surveys linking late-night eating to weight gain may be misleading because","choices":["the people who eat at night may also choose higher-calorie foods or eat more overall.","controlled studies found that nighttime eating increases metabolism.","participants in the surveys ate only in the morning.","the surveys measured weight only once."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 says the link \"often reflects what people eat at night ... and how much they eat overall.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.37735822b0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-26","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Advocates of four-day school weeks argue that the schedule saves districts money on transportation and utilities and gives teachers more time to plan.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In several rural districts that adopted four-day weeks, savings turned out to be smaller than expected, typically a few percent of the budget. Some families also reported difficulty finding child care on the fifth day. Although teachers generally liked the schedule, results for student achievement were mixed across districts.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By noting that the savings may be modest and that the schedule can create other challenges","By claiming that four-day weeks increase transportation costs","By arguing that teachers dislike four-day weeks","By agreeing that four-day weeks clearly improve student achievement"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 reports small savings, child-care problems, and mixed achievement results. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.782505cd61","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-27","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many birdwatchers believe that feeding wild birds in winter helps them survive the cold months by providing extra energy when natural food is scarce.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Winter feeding can help some birds survive, but it can also have unintended effects. In one region, feeders encouraged certain species to stay farther north instead of migrating, and crowds at feeders helped spread diseases among birds. Researchers suggest keeping feeders clean and spacing them apart to reduce these risks.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that feeding birds always causes them to die","By suggesting that feeders should be placed close together","By agreeing that feeding can help birds survive while warning that it can also cause problems that should be managed","By arguing that birds never need extra food in winter"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 2 grants that feeding \"can help\" but notes changed migration and disease, and recommends precautions. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e879c5905c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data2-27","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many birdwatchers believe that feeding wild birds in winter helps them survive the cold months by providing extra energy when natural food is scarce.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Winter feeding can help some birds survive, but it can also have unintended effects. In one region, feeders encouraged certain species to stay farther north instead of migrating, and crowds at feeders helped spread diseases among birds. Researchers suggest keeping feeders clean and spacing them apart to reduce these risks.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes how Text 2 relates to Text 1?","choices":["It qualifies the view in Text 1 by describing possible drawbacks of the practice.","It describes a different practice unrelated to Text 1.","It provides evidence that the view in Text 1 is entirely mistaken.","It restates the view in Text 1 in more technical language."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 accepts the benefit but adds risks and precautions. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.32dd339689","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-passage_data8-176","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers claim that eating fat makes people gain weight more than eating any other nutrient.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Fat contains more calories per gram than protein or carbohydrates, but studies comparing low-fat and low-carbohydrate diets with equal calories have found similar weight loss on average. What matters most for weight appears to be total calories and whether people can stick with a diet over time.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that when calories are equal, low-fat diets do not produce more weight loss, and that total calories and adherence matter most","By agreeing that fat causes more weight gain than any other nutrient regardless of calories","By arguing that diets should contain no carbohydrates","By claiming that fat contains fewer calories than protein"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Equal-calorie diets produced similar results. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.db78249d46","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-306","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Supporters of homework-free policies in elementary schools argue that homework adds stress without improving learning, and that children benefit more from play and family time after school.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research on elementary homework shows mixed results. Short, well-designed assignments, such as reading with a parent for fifteen minutes, have been linked to gains in reading. Long worksheets that repeat class material show little benefit and can cause frustration. The question, according to many researchers, is not whether to assign homework but what kind to assign.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the supporters in Text 1 that","choices":["family time should be replaced by homework.","play is harmful to children.","all homework should be banned.","some homework can cause frustration without improving learning."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Text 2 says long worksheets \"can cause frustration.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.00189cb557","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-308","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that audiobooks are a lesser form of reading, claiming that listeners absorb less of a book's meaning than people who read print.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In one study, participants who listened to a short story and participants who read it scored similarly on questions about plot and character. Listeners actually recalled more details about characters' emotions, which the researchers attributed to narrators' vocal expression. The researchers noted, however, that readers found it easier to reread difficult passages.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, listeners recalled characters' emotions better most likely because","choices":["listeners took notes while listening.","listeners reread difficult passages.","narrators conveyed emotion through their voices.","the story was shorter for listeners."],"answer":"C","explanation":"The researchers \"attributed\" it to \"narrators' vocal expression.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.7339fa98d7","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-310","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Advocates of standing desks claim that working while standing burns far more calories than sitting and can help office workers lose weight.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Standing does burn slightly more energy than sitting, but measurements suggest the difference is modest, roughly the equivalent of a small snack over an entire workday. Standing desks may reduce back discomfort for some workers, but they are unlikely on their own to produce significant weight loss.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that sitting burns more calories than standing","By acknowledging that standing burns somewhat more calories while arguing that the difference is too small to cause much weight loss","By arguing that standing desks always cause back pain","By agreeing that standing desks lead to major weight loss"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Text 2 grants a small increase but disputes significant weight loss. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4b17d91072","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-310","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Advocates of standing desks claim that working while standing burns far more calories than sitting and can help office workers lose weight.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Standing does burn slightly more energy than sitting, but measurements suggest the difference is modest, roughly the equivalent of a small snack over an entire workday. Standing desks may reduce back discomfort for some workers, but they are unlikely on their own to produce significant weight loss.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a possible benefit of standing desks is that they","choices":["may reduce back discomfort for some workers.","replace the need for exercise.","make workers more productive in every case.","cause substantial weight loss."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"May reduce back discomfort for some workers.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.bea42e3043","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-311","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some economists argue that raising taxes on sugary drinks will reduce consumption, because higher prices discourage buyers.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>After one city added a tax on sugary drinks, sales within the city fell sharply. However, sales at stores just outside the city limits rose, as some shoppers crossed the border to buy cheaper drinks. Overall consumption among residents dropped, but by less than the in-city sales figures alone suggested.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, in-city sales figures alone would most likely have","choices":["exaggerated how much residents' consumption fell.","underestimated how much residents' consumption fell.","shown that sales rose.","matched overall consumption exactly."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Some purchases shifted outside the city. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.409c827730","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-313","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Proponents of year-round schooling argue that the long summer break causes students to forget much of what they learned, forcing teachers to spend weeks reviewing each fall.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of year-round calendars, which spread the same number of school days across shorter breaks, have found only small differences in overall achievement compared with traditional calendars. Some evidence suggests that summer learning loss is largest among students from low-income families, who may have fewer educational opportunities during the break.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a year-round calendar differs from a traditional calendar mainly in","choices":["the subjects that are taught.","how school days are distributed across the year.","the total number of school days.","the age at which students begin school."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It \"spread[s] the same number of school days across shorter breaks.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6286141f09","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-315","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Museum curators have traditionally argued that fragile artifacts should never be handled by visitors, since even careful touching can cause gradual damage.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Some museums now use high-quality replicas, created from detailed scans, that visitors are encouraged to touch. Surveys suggest that handling replicas helps visitors understand an object's weight and texture, and the originals remain safely behind glass. Curators who once opposed hands-on displays have found that replicas address their main concern.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, handling replicas benefits visitors by helping them","choices":["avoid visiting museums.","repair damaged artifacts.","understand objects' weight and texture.","identify which objects are fake."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Surveys suggest replicas help with \"weight and texture.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.60e43a8a39","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-315","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Museum curators have traditionally argued that fragile artifacts should never be handled by visitors, since even careful touching can cause gradual damage.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Some museums now use high-quality replicas, created from detailed scans, that visitors are encouraged to touch. Surveys suggest that handling replicas helps visitors understand an object's weight and texture, and the originals remain safely behind glass. Curators who once opposed hands-on displays have found that replicas address their main concern.</p>","prompt":"The curators described in Text 2 would most likely say that replicas","choices":["address the concern expressed in Text 1.","should never be touched.","cause more damage than originals.","are less accurate than photographs."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Replicas \"address their main concern.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.daa1e715df","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-317","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many writers claim that people have become worse at paying attention because of smartphones, citing a widely repeated claim that human attention spans are now shorter than a goldfish's.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Researchers who have searched for the source of the goldfish comparison have been unable to find any study supporting it. Attention also varies greatly by task; people who struggle to focus on a dull lecture may concentrate for hours on a game or a novel. Whether smartphones affect attention is a legitimate question, but the goldfish claim is not evidence.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that the goldfish comparison is unsupported, even though the question about smartphones deserves study","By suggesting that people cannot concentrate on novels","By claiming that smartphones improve attention","By agreeing that attention spans are shorter than a goldfish's"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 rejects the statistic but keeps the question open. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.17d46e2821","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-317","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many writers claim that people have become worse at paying attention because of smartphones, citing a widely repeated claim that human attention spans are now shorter than a goldfish's.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Researchers who have searched for the source of the goldfish comparison have been unable to find any study supporting it. Attention also varies greatly by task; people who struggle to focus on a dull lecture may concentrate for hours on a game or a novel. Whether smartphones affect attention is a legitimate question, but the goldfish claim is not evidence.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the example of people concentrating for hours on a game suggests that","choices":["lectures are more engaging than games.","attention depends on the task, not a single fixed span.","games damage attention.","people cannot focus for long periods."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Attention also varies greatly by task.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.dcfc64bfc3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-318","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some architects argue that tall glass skyscrapers are energy-efficient because they let in abundant daylight, reducing the need for electric lighting.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Glass walls do admit daylight, but they also let heat escape in winter and absorb heat in summer, often requiring powerful heating and cooling systems. Studies of office towers have found that fully glazed buildings frequently use more total energy than buildings with a mix of windows and insulated walls, even after lighting savings are counted.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the architects in Text 1 that glass walls","choices":["let in daylight.","prevent heat from escaping.","keep buildings cool in summer.","reduce total energy use."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Glass walls do admit daylight.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.53ccf08ccc","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-319","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many coaches have long believed that athletes should stretch before exercise to prevent injuries.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Several large studies of runners and military recruits found that stretching before exercise did little to reduce injury rates. Some research suggests that a dynamic warm-up, such as light jogging and movement drills, prepares muscles more effectively than holding static stretches. Stretching may still improve flexibility over time when done regularly.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that static stretching prevents most injuries","By arguing that flexibility has no value","By presenting evidence that pre-exercise stretching does little to prevent injuries and suggesting a different warm-up","By claiming that warming up increases injuries"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Studies found little reduction in injuries; dynamic warm-ups may work better. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.8dd4b7d47c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-319","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many coaches have long believed that athletes should stretch before exercise to prevent injuries.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Several large studies of runners and military recruits found that stretching before exercise did little to reduce injury rates. Some research suggests that a dynamic warm-up, such as light jogging and movement drills, prepares muscles more effectively than holding static stretches. Stretching may still improve flexibility over time when done regularly.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 confirms the belief described in Text 1.","Text 2 challenges a long-held belief described in Text 1.","Text 2 and Text 1 discuss unrelated sports.","Text 2 describes the history of coaching."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Evidence contradicts the traditional practice. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.7af099fbf6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-320","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians have argued that a small ancient kingdom collapsed suddenly because of a single devastating invasion.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Excavations at the kingdom's cities reveal a more gradual process. Layers of soil show declining harvests over several decades, and records mention repeated droughts and rising grain prices. The invasion occurred, but it struck a kingdom already weakened by years of hardship.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that the invasion alone caused the collapse","By arguing that the kingdom was already weakened by long-term problems before the invasion occurred","By claiming that no invasion ever took place","By suggesting that the kingdom grew stronger before the invasion"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Text 2 describes gradual decline plus the invasion. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6acb7b2e1b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-320","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians have argued that a small ancient kingdom collapsed suddenly because of a single devastating invasion.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Excavations at the kingdom's cities reveal a more gradual process. Layers of soil show declining harvests over several decades, and records mention repeated droughts and rising grain prices. The invasion occurred, but it struck a kingdom already weakened by years of hardship.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which evidence suggests a gradual decline?","choices":["Soil layers showing declining harvests over decades","A single record of the invasion","Weapons found in one city","A description of the kingdom's founding"],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Declining harvests over several decades.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.dd7032c5be","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-320","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians have argued that a small ancient kingdom collapsed suddenly because of a single devastating invasion.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Excavations at the kingdom's cities reveal a more gradual process. Layers of soil show declining harvests over several decades, and records mention repeated droughts and rising grain prices. The invasion occurred, but it struck a kingdom already weakened by years of hardship.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the historians in Text 1 that","choices":["grain prices fell.","the collapse happened in a single day.","an invasion occurred.","droughts were unimportant."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"The invasion occurred.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9c2dbf0500","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-322","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that lightning never strikes the same place twice.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Tall structures and high points in a landscape are struck repeatedly. Some skyscrapers are hit dozens of times each year, and lightning rods are designed precisely because tall objects attract strikes again and again. The saying may be meant as encouragement, but it is not accurate.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions skyscrapers primarily to","choices":["give an example that contradicts the belief in Text 1.","describe the history of lightning rods.","explain how lightning forms.","argue that skyscrapers are unsafe."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Skyscrapers are struck \"dozens of times each year.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.3fe734e4ae","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-323","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Advocates of organic farming argue that it is better for the environment in every respect than conventional farming.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Organic farms often support greater biodiversity and use fewer synthetic pesticides. However, they typically produce smaller harvests per acre, which means more land may be needed to grow the same amount of food. Whether organic farming is better overall can depend on how much land is available and which environmental impacts are measured.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By acknowledging environmental benefits of organic farming while noting drawbacks that make its overall impact more complicated","By agreeing that organic farming is better in every respect","By arguing that biodiversity does not matter","By claiming that organic farms use more synthetic pesticides"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 cites benefits and lower yields. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.1ac7557286","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-325","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>A popular idea holds that people are either \"left-brained,\" meaning logical and analytical, or \"right-brained,\" meaning creative and intuitive.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Brain imaging studies show that both hemispheres are active during nearly all complex tasks, including math and art. Some functions, such as aspects of language, rely more on one side, but researchers have found no evidence that individuals consistently use one hemisphere more than the other.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 describes a different theory of creativity.","Text 2 expands on the popular idea in Text 1.","Text 2 uses research to reject the popular idea in Text 1.","Text 2 argues that the idea in Text 1 is proven."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Text 2 finds no evidence for hemispheric types. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c49805c234","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-326","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that video games are a waste of time with no educational value.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research on certain action games has found that players can improve in tracking multiple moving objects and switching attention quickly. Puzzle and strategy games may encourage planning and problem solving. The benefits depend heavily on the type of game, and researchers caution that many popular games have not been shown to teach specific skills.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely say that the critics' claim is","choices":["irrelevant to research on games.","correct about puzzle games only.","too broad, because some games may have educational value.","fully supported by research."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Some games show benefits. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.64f1aa337e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-327","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some city officials argue that planting trees along streets is mainly a way to make neighborhoods more attractive.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Street trees provide measurable benefits beyond appearance. Studies have linked tree-lined streets to cooler summer temperatures, reduced stormwater runoff, and in some neighborhoods, lower energy costs for nearby homes. Researchers suggest that trees should be treated as public infrastructure, like sidewalks and drainage systems.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, both authors would most likely agree that street trees","choices":["can make neighborhoods more attractive.","increase stormwater runoff.","raise summer temperatures.","have no measurable effects."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 says benefits go \"beyond appearance,\" implying appearance is one benefit. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.8a535c4871","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-328","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that bats are blind.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>All bat species can see, and some large fruit bats have excellent vision suited to finding food at dusk. Many smaller bats rely heavily on echolocation, emitting sounds and listening to echoes to navigate and hunt in darkness, which may be why people assume bats cannot see.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, large fruit bats are notable because they","choices":["rely entirely on echolocation.","cannot see at all.","have excellent vision.","hunt insects underwater."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Some large fruit bats have excellent vision.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ea68423e59","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data1-329","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some business leaders argue that open-plan offices, without walls or private offices, encourage collaboration by making it easy for employees to talk.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A study that tracked employees before and after two companies switched to open-plan offices found that face-to-face interaction actually decreased by about seventy percent, while email and messaging increased. Researchers suggest that employees, lacking privacy, may have used headphones and digital messages to avoid being overheard or interrupted.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 describes the architecture of office buildings.","Text 2 summarizes Text 1 without adding evidence.","Text 2 confirms the expectation in Text 1.","Text 2 reports a result contrary to the expectation in Text 1."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Collaboration was expected to rise but face-to-face contact fell. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.cd36a818f8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-330","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some conservation groups argue that zoos mainly serve entertainment and do little for wild animal populations.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Many accredited zoos run breeding programs for endangered species, and several species that had vanished from the wild have been reintroduced from zoo-bred populations. Zoos also fund field research and habitat protection. Critics are right that not all zoos meet high standards, but well-run zoos can make meaningful contributions to conservation.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that well-run zoos contribute to conservation through breeding, research, and habitat protection, while conceding that some zoos fall short","By suggesting that endangered species should never be bred in captivity","By agreeing that zoos do nothing for wild populations","By claiming that all zoos meet high standards"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 cites breeding and funding while granting that not all zoos are good. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6e4dcde058","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-331","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some technology writers predict that printed books will soon disappear, replaced entirely by e-books.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>E-book sales grew rapidly for several years after e-readers were introduced, but they later leveled off, and print books continue to account for a large share of sales. Surveys suggest that many readers value the physical experience of print, while others prefer e-books for travel. The two formats appear likely to coexist.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, some readers prefer e-books because they","choices":["cost more than print books.","offer a physical reading experience.","cannot be read on e-readers.","are convenient for travel."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Others prefer e-books for travel.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.b84c69b0b6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-331","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some technology writers predict that printed books will soon disappear, replaced entirely by e-books.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>E-book sales grew rapidly for several years after e-readers were introduced, but they later leveled off, and print books continue to account for a large share of sales. Surveys suggest that many readers value the physical experience of print, while others prefer e-books for travel. The two formats appear likely to coexist.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 supports the prediction in Text 1.","Text 2 agrees that print sales have ended.","Text 2 discusses the history of printing.","Text 2 presents evidence against the prediction in Text 1."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Sales leveled off and print endures. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5999d9c1c9","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-333","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some farmers argue that bees are the only pollinators that matter for crops.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Honeybees and wild bees are important, but studies of crop fields around the world show that flies, beetles, moths, and butterflies also pollinate many crops. In some fields, these insects account for a substantial share of flower visits. Protecting a variety of pollinators may make crop production more resilient if bee populations decline.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, protecting a variety of pollinators could be valuable because","choices":["other insects produce honey.","moths are easier to raise than bees.","bees would stop visiting flowers.","crops would depend less on bees alone if bee numbers fell."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Diversity makes production \"more resilient.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a50f5d55b3","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-334","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics claim that remote learning is always less effective than in-person instruction.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Results from remote courses vary widely. Studies suggest that well-designed online courses with frequent interaction, clear structure, and regular feedback can produce learning outcomes similar to in-person courses. Poorly designed courses, and students without reliable internet or quiet study spaces, often fare worse.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that remote learning's effectiveness depends on course design and students' circumstances","By agreeing that remote learning is always less effective","By suggesting that feedback harms learning","By claiming that online courses are always more effective"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Outcomes vary by design and student conditions. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.0f9c17e62e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-337","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many runners believe that expensive, highly cushioned running shoes prevent injuries.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of runners wearing different kinds of shoes have not consistently found that cushioning reduces injury rates. Some evidence suggests that choosing a shoe that feels comfortable to the individual runner may matter more than the amount of cushioning or the price.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that evidence does not clearly show that cushioning prevents injuries and that comfort may matter more","By agreeing that expensive shoes always prevent injuries","By suggesting that runners should run barefoot","By claiming that cushioning causes injuries"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Studies are inconsistent; comfort may matter most. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.342ff1d26b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-337","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many runners believe that expensive, highly cushioned running shoes prevent injuries.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of runners wearing different kinds of shoes have not consistently found that cushioning reduces injury rates. Some evidence suggests that choosing a shoe that feels comfortable to the individual runner may matter more than the amount of cushioning or the price.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a runner trying to reduce injury risk would most likely be advised to","choices":["buy the most expensive shoes available.","choose shoes that feel comfortable to them.","avoid wearing shoes.","select the most cushioned shoes."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Comfort \"may matter more.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4a19ed1341","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-337","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many runners believe that expensive, highly cushioned running shoes prevent injuries.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of runners wearing different kinds of shoes have not consistently found that cushioning reduces injury rates. Some evidence suggests that choosing a shoe that feels comfortable to the individual runner may matter more than the amount of cushioning or the price.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions price mainly to","choices":["recommend buying shoes on sale.","suggest that a shoe's cost is not a reliable guide to injury prevention.","explain why cushioned shoes are expensive.","argue that cheap shoes cause injuries."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Comfort may matter more \"than ... the price.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a14e26de60","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-338","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban designers argue that pedestrian-only shopping streets always attract more customers than streets open to cars.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Many pedestrian streets have thrived, especially in dense city centers with heavy foot traffic. But in some smaller towns, closing main streets to cars reduced visits, as shoppers who arrived by car found parking too distant. Several of these towns later reopened their streets to traffic. Success appears to depend on local conditions.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that pedestrian streets always attract more customers","By claiming that no pedestrian street has ever succeeded","By suggesting that all towns should close streets to cars","By arguing that pedestrian streets succeed in some places but not others, depending on local conditions"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Dense centers thrive; some small towns struggled. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.2141e7a4bb","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-338","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban designers argue that pedestrian-only shopping streets always attract more customers than streets open to cars.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Many pedestrian streets have thrived, especially in dense city centers with heavy foot traffic. But in some smaller towns, closing main streets to cars reduced visits, as shoppers who arrived by car found parking too distant. Several of these towns later reopened their streets to traffic. Success appears to depend on local conditions.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, some small towns reopened streets to traffic most likely because","choices":["the streets were too narrow for walking.","foot traffic increased too much.","pedestrians demanded more parking lots.","closing them to cars had reduced shopper visits."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Visits declined when parking was distant. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.d9a720986f","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-339","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists have argued that dinosaurs were wiped out slowly by gradual climate change over millions of years.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A layer of rock found around the world marks the end of the age of dinosaurs. It contains unusually high levels of iridium, an element rare on Earth but common in asteroids, and a large impact crater of the right age has been identified. Many scientists conclude that an asteroid impact caused a rapid mass extinction, though some argue that volcanic eruptions had already stressed ecosystems.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that the extinction happened only through gradual climate change","By presenting evidence pointing to a sudden asteroid impact, while noting that other factors may also have played a role","By claiming that dinosaurs survived the asteroid impact","By arguing that iridium is common on Earth"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Iridium layer and crater support a rapid event. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e2147804df","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-339","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists have argued that dinosaurs were wiped out slowly by gradual climate change over millions of years.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A layer of rock found around the world marks the end of the age of dinosaurs. It contains unusually high levels of iridium, an element rare on Earth but common in asteroids, and a large impact crater of the right age has been identified. Many scientists conclude that an asteroid impact caused a rapid mass extinction, though some argue that volcanic eruptions had already stressed ecosystems.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the iridium in the rock layer is significant because","choices":["iridium is found only in dinosaur bones.","iridium is common in asteroids but rare on Earth.","iridium forms slowly over millions of years.","iridium causes volcanic eruptions."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It suggests an extraterrestrial source. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.3f94367eb8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-339","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists have argued that dinosaurs were wiped out slowly by gradual climate change over millions of years.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A layer of rock found around the world marks the end of the age of dinosaurs. It contains unusually high levels of iridium, an element rare on Earth but common in asteroids, and a large impact crater of the right age has been identified. Many scientists conclude that an asteroid impact caused a rapid mass extinction, though some argue that volcanic eruptions had already stressed ecosystems.</p>","prompt":"The mention of volcanic eruptions in Text 2 suggests that","choices":["the author rejects the asteroid explanation.","the asteroid never struck Earth.","volcanoes produce iridium layers.","the extinction may have had more than one contributing cause."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Volcanoes \"had already stressed ecosystems.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a803c72a1e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-340","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some people believe that reading on screens damages eyesight permanently.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Long periods of screen use can cause temporary eye strain, dryness, and blurred vision, often because people blink less while staring at screens. Eye doctors recommend regular breaks, such as looking at something distant every twenty minutes. There is little evidence, however, that screen use by itself causes permanent damage to adult vision.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, screen use contributes to eye strain partly because people","choices":["take too many breaks.","sit too far from screens.","blink less while looking at screens.","read printed books too often."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"People blink less while staring at screens.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.add45b0e31","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-341","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some economists argue that automation will eliminate most jobs within a few decades.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Past waves of automation, such as the mechanization of farming, eliminated many specific jobs but also created new types of work, from factory employment to services. Automation can cause serious hardship for workers whose skills become obsolete, but history suggests that total employment has tended to recover as economies adapt, though often slowly and unevenly.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that automation never causes hardship","By suggesting that farming was never mechanized","By arguing that automation historically created new work even as it eliminated some jobs, though transitions can be difficult","By agreeing that automation will eliminate most jobs"],"answer":"C","explanation":"History shows job loss and creation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9a2c740e28","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-342","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some coaches argue that young athletes should specialize in a single sport early to develop elite skills.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of college and professional athletes found that many played several sports during childhood and specialized only in their teens. Early specialization has been linked to higher rates of overuse injuries and burnout, while playing multiple sports may develop a broader range of physical skills.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 supports the recommendation in Text 1.","Text 2 agrees but adds that coaches should train more.","Text 2 discusses professional contracts.","Text 2 challenges the recommendation in Text 1 with evidence about athletes' development."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Evidence counters early specialization. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4cb11e3945","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-344","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some residents oppose building apartment complexes in their neighborhoods, arguing that new housing will lower nearby property values.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of several cities found that new apartment buildings generally had little effect on nearby home values, and in some cases values rose slightly as new residents supported local shops and services. Effects varied, but the fear of widespread declines was not supported by most of the evidence.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, property values sometimes rose near new apartment buildings because","choices":["homeowners renovated their houses.","the apartments were demolished.","fewer people lived in the neighborhood.","new residents supported local shops and services."],"answer":"D","explanation":"New residents \"supported local shops and services.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5058b2ae35","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-345","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that dogs feel guilty when they misbehave, pointing to the \"guilty look\" dogs show when owners discover a mess.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>In one experiment, owners left the room, and researchers either had the dog eat a forbidden treat or removed it themselves. Owners were then told, sometimes falsely, that their dog had misbehaved. Dogs showed the \"guilty look\" most often when owners scolded them, regardless of whether they had actually eaten the treat, suggesting the look responds to the owner's behavior rather than to guilt.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 restates Text 1.","Text 2 offers evidence for a different explanation of the behavior described in Text 1.","Text 2 describes a different animal.","Text 2 confirms the explanation in Text 1."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The look reflects owners' behavior, not guilt. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.b6074570cf","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-347","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents believe that children who watch educational television learn as much as children who read books.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of young children suggest that well-designed educational programs can teach vocabulary and early math concepts. However, children tend to learn more when adults watch with them and discuss what they see, and shared book reading exposes children to a wider range of vocabulary than most television programs do.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that children learn nothing from television","By suggesting that adults should never watch television with children","By agreeing that television teaches exactly as much as books","By acknowledging that educational programs can teach some skills while suggesting that shared reading offers additional benefits"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Programs help, but reading exposes children to more vocabulary. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.f2e8d50ac1","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-347","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents believe that children who watch educational television learn as much as children who read books.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of young children suggest that well-designed educational programs can teach vocabulary and early math concepts. However, children tend to learn more when adults watch with them and discuss what they see, and shared book reading exposes children to a wider range of vocabulary than most television programs do.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the parents in Text 1 that","choices":["television is better than reading.","educational television can help children learn.","children should avoid educational programs.","books have no educational value."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Programs \"can teach vocabulary and early math concepts.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.4100b312aa","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-349","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some art critics dismiss street art as vandalism with no artistic merit.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Unauthorized graffiti can damage property, and many cities prohibit it. Yet some street artists create commissioned murals that neighborhoods welcome, and several museums have exhibited works by artists who began on the street. Whether a piece counts as vandalism or art may depend on permission, context, and community response.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, commissioned murals differ from vandalism mainly because they","choices":["are created with permission and welcomed by communities.","are always painted indoors.","are created only by museum employees.","use different kinds of paint."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Permission and community response matter. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.88e9b00889","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-350","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some educators argue that students learn best when teachers explain concepts directly before students practice.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Other educators favor having students attempt a problem before receiving instruction. Studies suggest that struggling with a problem first can help students notice what they do not understand, making the later explanation more meaningful. However, this approach works best when students have enough background knowledge to make progress; complete beginners may simply become frustrated.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that direct explanation should always come first for every student","By claiming that instruction is unnecessary","By arguing that frustration always improves learning","By suggesting that attempting problems before instruction can help, though only when students have sufficient background knowledge"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Productive struggle helps, with a caveat for beginners. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a6c09690e6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-351","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some ecologists argue that all non-native species should be removed from protected areas.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Many non-native species cause serious harm, outcompeting native plants and animals. But some have become integrated into ecosystems in ways that benefit native wildlife; certain non-native shrubs, for example, now provide nesting sites for endangered birds where native vegetation has disappeared. Managers may need to evaluate each species' effects rather than applying a single rule.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions non-native shrubs mainly to","choices":["argue that birds should be relocated.","show that shrubs outcompete all native plants.","give an example of a non-native species that benefits native wildlife.","explain how shrubs were introduced."],"answer":"C","explanation":"They provide nesting sites for endangered birds. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.45fc95b626","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-352","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers claim that drinking eight glasses of water a day is necessary for good health.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The body's water needs vary with climate, activity, and body size, and much of the water people need comes from food and other beverages. Most healthy adults can rely on thirst to guide how much they drink. The eight-glass rule may be a useful reminder for some people but is not a scientific requirement for everyone.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that water needs vary and that the eight-glass rule is not a universal requirement","By claiming that people need no water","By suggesting that food contains no water","By agreeing that everyone must drink exactly eight glasses"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Needs vary; thirst is usually a reliable guide. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.88ccbecee7","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-352","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers claim that drinking eight glasses of water a day is necessary for good health.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The body's water needs vary with climate, activity, and body size, and much of the water people need comes from food and other beverages. Most healthy adults can rely on thirst to guide how much they drink. The eight-glass rule may be a useful reminder for some people but is not a scientific requirement for everyone.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, much of the water people need comes from","choices":["food and other beverages.","sweat.","only plain water.","medical supplements."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Much of the water people need comes from food and other beverages.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6f5cb5acf6","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-353","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians argue that the invention of the printing press immediately transformed European society.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Printing did make books cheaper and more widely available, but literacy rates rose slowly, and for decades most printed works were religious texts and classical writings already familiar to scholars. The press's broader social effects unfolded over generations as schooling expanded and new kinds of writing, such as newspapers, developed.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the historians in Text 1 that the printing press","choices":["made books more widely available.","was invented after newspapers.","reduced literacy.","had no effect on society."],"answer":"A","explanation":"\"Printing did make books cheaper and more widely available.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e6c2645f32","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-354","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some psychologists argue that people make better decisions when they carefully analyze every option.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>For complex choices with many factors, such as choosing an apartment, some studies have found that people who thought carefully and then took a break before deciding made choices they were later more satisfied with. For simple decisions, careful analysis works well. Deliberation is valuable, but the best approach may depend on the type of decision.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that simple decisions require breaks","By agreeing that careful analysis is best for every decision","By suggesting that the best decision-making approach may depend on the complexity of the decision","By claiming that people should never think about decisions"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The approach depends on the decision type. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.32c2c02daa","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data2-355","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that goldfish have memories lasting only a few seconds.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Experiments have shown that goldfish can be trained to respond to sounds or colors associated with feeding and can remember these associations for months. Some goldfish have learned to push levers for food at particular times of day. Their memory is far better than the popular belief suggests.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions goldfish pushing levers mainly to","choices":["explain how aquariums are cleaned.","provide an additional example of goldfish learning.","show that goldfish are aggressive.","argue that goldfish should not be fed."],"answer":"B","explanation":"It shows learning tied to times of day. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ccc60ea42c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-356","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that people learn best when information matches their preferred \"learning style,\" such as visual or auditory.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies that tested students' preferred styles and then taught them in matching or mismatching ways found little difference in learning. Students may prefer certain formats, but most people learn more from combining words, images, and active practice than from a single mode. Researchers suggest that the content being taught, not an individual's supposed style, should guide instruction.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with Text 1 that","choices":["learning styles determine how much students learn.","auditory learners cannot learn from images.","students may prefer certain formats of information.","instruction should use only one mode."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Students may prefer certain formats.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.8c1f93342e","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-359","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that audiences prefer films based on original stories rather than sequels and remakes.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Box office data from recent decades show that sequels and remakes have frequently been among the highest-earning films each year. Surveys, however, suggest that many viewers say they want more original films, even as they buy tickets to familiar franchises. What audiences say and what they choose may differ.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely say that the critics' claim in Text 1","choices":["reflects what many viewers say but not what box office data show.","ignores surveys of viewers.","applies only to animated films.","is fully supported by box office data."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Surveys support it; ticket sales do not. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.2df2788028","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-361","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians describe a famous explorer as the first person to reach a particular region of North America.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Indigenous peoples had lived in the region for thousands of years before the explorer arrived, and oral histories and archaeological sites document their long presence. The explorer's journey was significant for European knowledge of the area, but describing him as the first to reach it overlooks the people who already lived there.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that archaeological sites are unreliable","By claiming that the explorer never traveled to the region","By agreeing that the explorer was the first person in the region","By pointing out that Indigenous peoples had lived in the region long before the explorer arrived"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Indigenous presence predates the explorer. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.1a3d280932","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-361","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians describe a famous explorer as the first person to reach a particular region of North America.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Indigenous peoples had lived in the region for thousands of years before the explorer arrived, and oral histories and archaeological sites document their long presence. The explorer's journey was significant for European knowledge of the area, but describing him as the first to reach it overlooks the people who already lived there.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 acknowledges that the explorer's journey","choices":["was the first human arrival in the region.","was significant for European knowledge of the region.","was invented by historians.","had no historical importance."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Significant for European knowledge of the area.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9822741178","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-362","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists argue that planting trees is one of the most effective ways to fight climate change.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Trees absorb carbon dioxide as they grow, and restoring forests can store significant amounts of carbon. However, new forests take decades to mature, planting in the wrong places can harm grasslands or water supplies, and trees can release stored carbon if they burn. Many researchers see tree planting as helpful but not a substitute for reducing emissions.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that tree planting alone will solve climate change","By arguing that forests should be cut down","By claiming that trees do not absorb carbon dioxide","By agreeing that trees store carbon while cautioning about limitations and arguing that planting cannot replace emission reductions"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Benefits plus limits; not a substitute. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e00a56cbbf","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-362","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists argue that planting trees is one of the most effective ways to fight climate change.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Trees absorb carbon dioxide as they grow, and restoring forests can store significant amounts of carbon. However, new forests take decades to mature, planting in the wrong places can harm grasslands or water supplies, and trees can release stored carbon if they burn. Many researchers see tree planting as helpful but not a substitute for reducing emissions.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, one risk of planting trees in the wrong places is that it can","choices":["prevent all forest fires.","harm grasslands or water supplies.","make trees grow faster.","reduce emissions from cars."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Planting in the wrong places \"can harm grasslands or water supplies.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.a055f0e3b5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-363","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Many people believe that humans use only ten percent of their brains.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Brain imaging shows activity throughout the brain, though not all regions are equally active at the same moment. Damage to almost any area of the brain can affect abilities, which would be unlikely if most of the brain went unused. Neuroscientists regard the ten-percent idea as a myth.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that brain imaging shows no activity","By agreeing that most of the brain is unused","By citing brain imaging and effects of brain damage as evidence that people use far more than ten percent of their brains","By arguing that brain damage has no effects"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Activity throughout the brain and effects of damage. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ffc55d5629","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-365","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some educators argue that grades motivate students to work hard.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Grades can encourage effort, especially for students focused on college admissions. But research suggests that when grades become the main goal, some students choose easier tasks to protect their scores and lose interest in learning for its own sake. Feedback that explains how to improve may sustain motivation more effectively for many students.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that grades never motivate anyone","By arguing that feedback reduces motivation","By agreeing that grades are the best motivation for all students","By acknowledging that grades can encourage effort while arguing that they can also undermine interest and challenge-seeking"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Grades help some but can have drawbacks. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.326b7239a8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-366","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians claim that medieval people believed the Earth was flat.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Educated writers throughout the Middle Ages generally accepted that the Earth was round, a view inherited from ancient Greek scholars. Textbooks used in medieval universities described a spherical Earth. The idea that medieval people commonly believed in a flat Earth became popular much later, partly through nineteenth-century writers.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that medieval people believed the Earth was flat","By arguing that universities did not exist in the Middle Ages","By arguing that educated medieval people generally knew the Earth was round and that the flat-Earth belief is a later myth","By claiming that ancient Greeks believed the Earth was flat"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Medieval textbooks described a spherical Earth. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.591b82bbc2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-366","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians claim that medieval people believed the Earth was flat.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Educated writers throughout the Middle Ages generally accepted that the Earth was round, a view inherited from ancient Greek scholars. Textbooks used in medieval universities described a spherical Earth. The idea that medieval people commonly believed in a flat Earth became popular much later, partly through nineteenth-century writers.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the idea that medieval people believed in a flat Earth","choices":["was accepted by all medieval scholars.","was proven by ancient Greeks.","became popular much later, partly through nineteenth-century writers.","came from medieval university textbooks."],"answer":"C","explanation":"It \"became popular much later.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.456f67aeeb","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-366","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians claim that medieval people believed the Earth was flat.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Educated writers throughout the Middle Ages generally accepted that the Earth was round, a view inherited from ancient Greek scholars. Textbooks used in medieval universities described a spherical Earth. The idea that medieval people commonly believed in a flat Earth became popular much later, partly through nineteenth-century writers.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions university textbooks mainly to","choices":["argue that universities were expensive.","provide evidence of what educated medieval people believed.","describe how books were printed.","show that textbooks contained errors."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Textbooks described a spherical Earth. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c0c439a2cb","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-367","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some people argue that working longer hours always leads to greater productivity.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of factory and office workers have found that output per hour tends to decline when work weeks become very long. Beyond a certain point, fatigue leads to more errors and accidents, so additional hours add little or even reduce total output. Many companies have found that moderate schedules with adequate rest sustain productivity better.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that longer hours always increase productivity","By presenting evidence that very long hours can reduce productivity because of fatigue","By arguing that rest reduces productivity","By claiming that workers never make errors"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Output per hour falls, and errors rise. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.84f40f99fe","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-368","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some critics argue that video calls can fully replace in-person meetings for business teams.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Video calls save travel time and allow teams in different cities to meet easily. However, surveys of workers report that brainstorming and building trust with new colleagues often feel more difficult over video, and informal conversations before and after meetings are less common. Many teams now mix video calls with occasional in-person gatherings.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that video calls waste time","By acknowledging the benefits of video calls while arguing that some kinds of collaboration work better in person","By arguing that teams should never meet in person","By agreeing that video calls fully replace in-person meetings"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Brainstorming and trust-building can suffer over video. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.f910bad842","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-369","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some nutrition writers argue that fresh vegetables are always more nutritious than frozen ones.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Vegetables are often frozen within hours of harvest, preserving many vitamins, while fresh vegetables may spend days in transport and storage, losing nutrients along the way. Studies comparing the two have found that frozen vegetables are often similar in nutrients to fresh ones and sometimes contain more of certain vitamins.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By agreeing that fresh vegetables are always more nutritious","By claiming that frozen vegetables contain no vitamins","By presenting evidence that frozen vegetables can be as nutritious as fresh ones, and sometimes more so","By arguing that vegetables should not be eaten"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Quick freezing preserves nutrients. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9115137db8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-370","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers say that the Industrial Revolution made life worse for ordinary workers.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Early factory work was often dangerous, hours were long, and crowded cities bred disease. Over time, however, rising productivity contributed to higher wages, cheaper goods, and eventually to reforms such as limits on child labor and working hours. The period's effects on workers changed considerably over the course of the century.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the writers in Text 1 that","choices":["cities were free of disease.","workers' lives never improved.","early factory work was often dangerous.","reforms made conditions worse."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Early factory work was often dangerous.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5fb4752f17","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-371","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists have claimed that birds' feathers first evolved to help them fly.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Fossils show that several dinosaurs that could not fly already had feathers. Some of these feathers were simple filaments that likely provided insulation, and others may have been used in displays to attract mates. Feathers may have been adapted for flight only later in the history of birds' ancestors.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 agrees that dinosaurs could fly.","Text 2 discusses the flight of insects.","Text 2 confirms that feathers evolved for flight.","Text 2 offers evidence suggesting a different origin for feathers than the one proposed in Text 1."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Feathers predate flight. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e5d49e4788","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-372","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents worry that children who have imaginary friends are lonely or have trouble making real friends.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies of preschool children have found that those with imaginary companions are generally as socially skilled as other children, and some show especially strong abilities to understand other people's perspectives. Imaginary friends appear to be a common and healthy part of development for many children.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that imaginary friends harm development","By agreeing that imaginary friends indicate loneliness","By presenting evidence that children with imaginary friends are typically socially skilled","By arguing that preschool children cannot make friends"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Studies show normal or strong social skills. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9def6eb6b0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-373","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some energy experts argue that nuclear power is too dangerous to be part of efforts to reduce carbon emissions.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Nuclear accidents have caused serious harm, and long-term storage of radioactive waste remains a challenge. Yet measured by deaths per unit of electricity produced, nuclear power has a safety record comparable to wind and solar and far better than coal. Some researchers argue that its risks should be weighed against the harms of fossil fuels.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, nuclear power's safety record, measured by deaths per unit of electricity, is","choices":["the worst of all energy sources.","impossible to measure.","comparable to wind and solar and better than coal.","worse than coal."],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Comparable to wind and solar and far better than coal.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.35d820cfab","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data3-375","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some sports commentators claim that home teams win more often mainly because of cheering crowds.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>When sports leagues played games without fans during a period of restrictions, the home advantage shrank in some sports but did not disappear. Researchers suggest that familiarity with the venue, reduced travel fatigue, and referees' decisions all contribute, with crowds being one factor among several.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with Text 1 that crowds","choices":["cause visiting teams to win.","may contribute to home advantage.","have no effect on games.","are the sole cause of home advantage."],"answer":"B","explanation":"Crowds are \"one factor among several.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.24990862a8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-376","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some commentators argue that social media has made teenagers less happy.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Large surveys find a small average association between heavy social media use and lower well-being among teenagers, but the effect varies widely. For some teens, especially those who use it passively for many hours, social media is linked to worse mood; others use it to maintain friendships and find support. Researchers caution that the average link is modest and does not show that social media causes unhappiness.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which teens are more likely to show a link between social media and worse mood?","choices":["Those who never use it","Those who use it to maintain friendships","Those who find support online","Those who use it passively for many hours"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Passive heavy use is linked to worse mood. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.3cd27fb1c8","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-376","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some commentators argue that social media has made teenagers less happy.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Large surveys find a small average association between heavy social media use and lower well-being among teenagers, but the effect varies widely. For some teens, especially those who use it passively for many hours, social media is linked to worse mood; others use it to maintain friendships and find support. Researchers caution that the average link is modest and does not show that social media causes unhappiness.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely say that the claim in Text 1","choices":["underestimates social media's harms.","overstates what the evidence shows.","ignores teenagers entirely.","is fully proven by surveys."],"answer":"B","explanation":"The link is modest and not causal. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e25b40075b","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-378","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some art historians argue that a famous painter worked alone and developed his distinctive style entirely by himself.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Workshop records show that the painter employed several assistants who prepared canvases, mixed pigments, and sometimes painted backgrounds. Letters also reveal that he studied and admired the works of earlier artists. His style was distinctive, but it developed within a network of collaborators and influences.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the art historians in Text 1 that the painter's style","choices":["did not exist.","was copied from his assistants.","was identical to earlier artists' styles.","was distinctive."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"His style was distinctive.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.26a3d0c9fe","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-378","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some art historians argue that a famous painter worked alone and developed his distinctive style entirely by himself.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Workshop records show that the painter employed several assistants who prepared canvases, mixed pigments, and sometimes painted backgrounds. Letters also reveal that he studied and admired the works of earlier artists. His style was distinctive, but it developed within a network of collaborators and influences.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the painter's assistants","choices":["signed his paintings.","never touched the canvases.","sometimes painted backgrounds.","taught him to paint."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Assistants \"sometimes painted backgrounds.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.85d54e18dd","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-381","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists claim that honeybees are the most important pollinators for wild plants.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Honeybees pollinate many crops, but studies of wild plant communities have found that native bees, such as bumblebees and solitary bees, often do more of the pollination. In some habitats, large honeybee colonies may even compete with native bees for flowers.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a possible concern about large honeybee colonies is that they","choices":["pollinate too few crops.","increase native bee populations.","may compete with native bees for flowers.","cannot find flowers."],"answer":"C","explanation":"They \"may even compete with native bees.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.cced6b7a19","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-382","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians argue that a certain ancient empire fell primarily because of economic problems.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The empire did suffer from inflation and heavy taxation, but it also faced repeated invasions, civil wars, and outbreaks of disease. Historians increasingly describe its decline as the result of many interacting pressures rather than a single cause.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By acknowledging economic problems while arguing that many other pressures also contributed to the empire's decline","By agreeing that economics was the only cause","By arguing that the empire never declined","By claiming that the empire had no economic problems"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Multiple interacting pressures. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.d5ad9632aa","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-382","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some historians argue that a certain ancient empire fell primarily because of economic problems.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>The empire did suffer from inflation and heavy taxation, but it also faced repeated invasions, civil wars, and outbreaks of disease. Historians increasingly describe its decline as the result of many interacting pressures rather than a single cause.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, which factor contributed to the empire's decline in addition to economic problems?","choices":["Declining taxes","Rapid population growth","Repeated invasions","A lack of civil wars"],"answer":"C","explanation":"\"Repeated invasions, civil wars, and outbreaks of disease.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.5d657256fd","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-383","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers argue that owning a pet improves people's health.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Some studies have linked dog ownership with more physical activity, since dog owners take regular walks. Other research finds that pet owners differ from non-owners in income, age, and lifestyle, which could explain some health differences. Pets may offer benefits, but the evidence that pets themselves improve health is mixed.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that dog owners never walk","By noting that some benefits have been linked to pets but that differences between owners and non-owners make the evidence mixed","By claiming that pets harm health","By agreeing that pets always improve health"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Benefits are possible, but confounders exist. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6bf6c68691","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-384","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some economists argue that raising the minimum wage always leads to large job losses.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies comparing neighboring regions with different minimum wage increases have often found small or no effects on employment for moderate increases. Researchers caution, however, that very large increases, or increases in areas with low average wages, might have different effects that are harder to predict.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that minimum wages should be eliminated","By claiming that minimum wage increases always create jobs","By presenting evidence that moderate increases often have small employment effects, while noting uncertainty about very large increases","By agreeing that any increase causes large job losses"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Small effects for moderate increases; uncertainty for large ones. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.6bb480aeb5","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-384","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some economists argue that raising the minimum wage always leads to large job losses.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies comparing neighboring regions with different minimum wage increases have often found small or no effects on employment for moderate increases. Researchers caution, however, that very large increases, or increases in areas with low average wages, might have different effects that are harder to predict.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, researchers are less certain about the effects of","choices":["very large minimum wage increases.","moderate increases in neighboring regions.","wages above the minimum.","increases that have already been studied."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Very large increases \"might have different effects.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.48d7f47f75","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-385","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some teachers believe that students should not use calculators until they have mastered arithmetic by hand.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research suggests that calculators, used thoughtfully, do not harm basic skills and can let students focus on problem solving and patterns. However, studies also show that students benefit from understanding arithmetic concepts and developing number sense before relying heavily on calculators.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the teachers in Text 1 that","choices":["number sense cannot be taught.","problem solving is unimportant.","students benefit from understanding arithmetic concepts.","calculators should never be used."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Students \"benefit from understanding arithmetic concepts.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.9cadbdc2b2","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-385","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some teachers believe that students should not use calculators until they have mastered arithmetic by hand.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research suggests that calculators, used thoughtfully, do not harm basic skills and can let students focus on problem solving and patterns. However, studies also show that students benefit from understanding arithmetic concepts and developing number sense before relying heavily on calculators.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, calculators used thoughtfully can allow students to","choices":["skip learning numbers.","avoid all math classes.","memorize answers without thinking.","focus on problem solving and patterns."],"answer":"D","explanation":"They let students \"focus on problem solving and patterns.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.3704b96651","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-386","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists argue that whales are too large to be affected by small plastic particles in the ocean.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Filter-feeding whales swallow enormous volumes of water to catch tiny prey, and researchers analyzing their feeding have estimated that some whales may ingest millions of microplastic particles daily. The long-term health effects are still being studied, but the scale of exposure suggests that size does not protect whales from plastic pollution.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that microplastics do not exist in oceans","By agreeing that whales are unaffected by plastic","By claiming that whales avoid eating prey","By arguing that whales' feeding methods may expose them to large amounts of microplastics despite their size"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Filter feeding leads to heavy exposure. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c582f31051","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-386","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists argue that whales are too large to be affected by small plastic particles in the ocean.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Filter-feeding whales swallow enormous volumes of water to catch tiny prey, and researchers analyzing their feeding have estimated that some whales may ingest millions of microplastic particles daily. The long-term health effects are still being studied, but the scale of exposure suggests that size does not protect whales from plastic pollution.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, filter-feeding whales may ingest many microplastics because they","choices":["live only near beaches.","swallow huge volumes of water while feeding.","eat plastic deliberately.","have very small mouths."],"answer":"B","explanation":"They \"swallow enormous volumes of water.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.8a3b63e386","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-386","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some scientists argue that whales are too large to be affected by small plastic particles in the ocean.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Filter-feeding whales swallow enormous volumes of water to catch tiny prey, and researchers analyzing their feeding have estimated that some whales may ingest millions of microplastic particles daily. The long-term health effects are still being studied, but the scale of exposure suggests that size does not protect whales from plastic pollution.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 acknowledges that","choices":["whales eat only large fish.","the long-term health effects are still being studied.","plastic pollution has ended.","microplastics are proven to kill all whales."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"The long-term health effects are still being studied.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.84118e1077","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-387","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that people who multitask are more productive.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Experiments show that switching between tasks takes time and increases errors, because the brain must reorient each time attention shifts. People who frequently multitask often perform worse on tests of attention than people who multitask less, although they tend to believe they are skilled at it.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, frequent multitaskers tend to","choices":["believe they are good at multitasking despite performing worse on attention tests.","avoid switching tasks.","perform better on attention tests.","make fewer errors than others."],"answer":"A","explanation":"They \"tend to believe they are skilled at it.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.27548eae7d","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-387","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some writers claim that people who multitask are more productive.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Experiments show that switching between tasks takes time and increases errors, because the brain must reorient each time attention shifts. People who frequently multitask often perform worse on tests of attention than people who multitask less, although they tend to believe they are skilled at it.</p>","prompt":"Which choice best describes the relationship between the texts?","choices":["Text 2 challenges the claim made in Text 1.","Text 2 agrees but adds that multitasking is fun.","Text 2 discusses computer processors.","Text 2 supports the claim made in Text 1."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Evidence counters productivity claims. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c49b72d002","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-388","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban officials argue that banning cars from city centers will harm local businesses.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>When several European cities closed central districts to most car traffic, some shops initially reported fewer customers. Within a few years, however, foot traffic increased in many of these districts, and surveys found that pedestrians often spent more time and money in shops than drivers had. Results varied by city and depended on transit access.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that shops had more customers immediately after every ban","By noting that early losses were often followed by increased foot traffic and spending, though results varied","By agreeing that car bans always harm businesses permanently","By arguing that transit access is irrelevant"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Initial dips, later gains, with variation. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.e2370d01ec","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-388","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban officials argue that banning cars from city centers will harm local businesses.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>When several European cities closed central districts to most car traffic, some shops initially reported fewer customers. Within a few years, however, foot traffic increased in many of these districts, and surveys found that pedestrians often spent more time and money in shops than drivers had. Results varied by city and depended on transit access.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, the success of car-free districts depended partly on","choices":["the number of parking lots.","the color of shop signs.","access to public transit.","banning pedestrians."],"answer":"C","explanation":"Results \"depended on transit access.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.671f9773b0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-388","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban officials argue that banning cars from city centers will harm local businesses.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>When several European cities closed central districts to most car traffic, some shops initially reported fewer customers. Within a few years, however, foot traffic increased in many of these districts, and surveys found that pedestrians often spent more time and money in shops than drivers had. Results varied by city and depended on transit access.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the officials in Text 1 that","choices":["all businesses close after car bans.","some shops may lose customers at first.","car bans have no effects.","pedestrians never shop."],"answer":"B","explanation":"\"Some shops initially reported fewer customers.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.793412d164","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-389","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some literary critics argue that a novel's meaning is determined entirely by what its author intended.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Authors' intentions can illuminate their works, and letters or interviews often reveal what writers hoped to achieve. Yet readers from different times and places frequently find meanings the author never anticipated, and many critics argue that these interpretations can be valid if they are supported by the text itself.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, a reader's interpretation is valid if it is","choices":["approved by the author's family.","the same as the author's intention.","published in a newspaper.","supported by the text itself."],"answer":"D","explanation":"Valid \"if they are supported by the text itself.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.56a58efafa","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-390","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some researchers argue that fish do not feel pain because they lack certain brain structures found in mammals.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Fish have nerve receptors that respond to harmful stimuli, and experiments show that fish exposed to such stimuli change their behavior, avoiding areas where they were harmed and responding to pain-relieving drugs. Although fish brains differ from mammal brains, some scientists argue that these findings suggest fish may experience something like pain.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that fish brains are identical to mammal brains","By arguing that pain-relieving drugs have no effect on fish","By presenting behavioral and physiological evidence that fish may experience something like pain despite brain differences","By agreeing that fish cannot feel pain"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Receptors, avoidance, and response to drugs. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.612110fe8d","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-391","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents believe that children should be praised for being smart to build their confidence.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies have found that children praised for intelligence were more likely to avoid challenging tasks afterward, perhaps fearing they would look less smart if they failed. Children praised for effort were more willing to try harder problems and persisted longer after mistakes.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that effort is unimportant","By presenting evidence that praising effort encourages persistence more than praising intelligence","By claiming that children should never be praised","By agreeing that praising intelligence always builds confidence"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Effort praise leads to more challenge-seeking. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.81d8cc91d0","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-391","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some parents believe that children should be praised for being smart to build their confidence.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Studies have found that children praised for intelligence were more likely to avoid challenging tasks afterward, perhaps fearing they would look less smart if they failed. Children praised for effort were more willing to try harder problems and persisted longer after mistakes.</p>","prompt":"Based on Text 2, children praised for intelligence may avoid challenges because they","choices":["find easy tasks boring.","prefer working in groups.","do not understand praise.","fear failing would make them seem less smart."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"Fearing they would look less smart.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.ced9fb32fd","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-393","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban planners argue that suburbs are always worse for the environment than cities.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>City residents generally drive less and live in smaller homes, which tends to reduce energy use per person. However, some suburbs with good transit and compact development have lower emissions than car-dependent areas within city limits. The environmental impact depends more on density, transit, and housing type than on whether an area is labeled urban or suburban.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claim in Text 1?","choices":["By claiming that cities always produce more emissions","By arguing that transit has no effect on emissions","By agreeing that every suburb is worse than every city neighborhood","By arguing that environmental impact depends on factors such as density and transit rather than simply on whether an area is a suburb"],"answer":"D","explanation":"Design matters more than labels. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.c7742f623c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-393","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some urban planners argue that suburbs are always worse for the environment than cities.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>City residents generally drive less and live in smaller homes, which tends to reduce energy use per person. However, some suburbs with good transit and compact development have lower emissions than car-dependent areas within city limits. The environmental impact depends more on density, transit, and housing type than on whether an area is labeled urban or suburban.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 would most likely agree with the planners in Text 1 that city residents","choices":["always live in larger homes.","produce more emissions than all suburbanites.","never use transit.","tend to drive less."],"answer":"D","explanation":"\"City residents generally drive less.\" The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.01aded767c","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"psg-crosstext_data4-395","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Some health writers argue that vitamin supplements are necessary for everyone.</p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>People with certain conditions, diets, or life stages may benefit from specific supplements; for example, pregnant women are often advised to take folic acid. For most healthy adults who eat a varied diet, however, large studies have found little benefit from routine multivitamins in preventing major diseases.</p>","prompt":"The author of Text 2 mentions folic acid for pregnant women mainly to","choices":["criticize doctors' advice.","give an example of a group that may benefit from a specific supplement.","argue that all supplements are unnecessary.","explain how vitamins are made."],"answer":"B","explanation":"An example of targeted benefit. The other choices either go beyond what the text says, contradict it, or focus on a detail rather than the point the question asks about."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.934a1b7171","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data3-18","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>For decades, scientists believed that the giant sequoias of California needed fire mainly to clear competing vegetation. <u>Fire, in this view, simply removed rivals, allowing sequoia seedlings access to sunlight.</u></p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>Research over the past fifty years has revealed a more intimate relationship. The heat of a fire dries sequoia cones and causes them to open and release seeds, and fire leaves behind a layer of ash-rich soil in which seedlings thrive. Where fires have been suppressed, sequoia groves have produced very few new trees, even in areas where competing vegetation was removed by hand.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?","choices":["The author of Text 2 would likely argue that the view is incomplete, since fire also directly aids sequoia reproduction in ways that clearing competitors alone does not.","The author of Text 2 would likely agree with the view, since sequoia groves produce few new trees where vegetation is not cleared.","The author of Text 2 would likely argue that the view is incorrect, since competing vegetation has no effect on the growth of sequoia seedlings.","The author of Text 2 would likely argue that the view overstates the importance of fire, since sequoias can reproduce well without it wherever competing vegetation has been removed by hand."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Text 2 says fire opens cones and creates good soil, and that clearing competitors by hand wasn't enough. So fire does more than remove rivals: the old view is incomplete. Text 2 doesn't say competition doesn't matter. It shows clearing isn't sufficient rather than agreeing. And it says groves without fire produce few new trees, the opposite of reproducing well without it."},
{"id":"g.rw.cross-text.3.0c39dc78d4","section":"rw","domain":"craft","skill":"cross-text","difficulty":3,"group":"hard-hard_data3-21","passage":"<p><b>Text 1</b></p><p>Researchers have long debated why some animals play. One influential hypothesis holds that <u>play serves primarily as practice for adult skills: young cats pounce on toys to rehearse hunting, and young goats butt heads to prepare for competition over mates.</u></p><p><b>Text 2</b></p><p>A study of meerkats tested this idea directly. Young meerkats that engaged in more play fighting did not become more successful fighters or more dominant adults than those that played less. The researchers suggest that play may instead help young animals cope with unexpected situations, since play fighting often involves sudden reversals and loss of balance.</p>","prompt":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?","choices":["The author of Text 2 would likely argue that the meerkat study casts doubt on the hypothesis and points toward a different benefit of play.","The author of Text 2 would likely argue that play has no benefits for young animals.","The author of Text 2 would likely argue that meerkats play less than young cats and goats do because adult meerkats rarely need to fight or hunt.","The author of Text 2 would likely argue that the meerkat study confirms the hypothesis for play fighting but not for other kinds of play."],"answer":"A","explanation":"Play fighting didn't produce better adult fighters, which contradicts the practice hypothesis, and the researchers suggest a different benefit (coping with the unexpected). The study doesn't confirm the hypothesis. It doesn't compare how much animals play, and it proposes a benefit rather than denying play has any."}
]);
